# Refactoring rule

Based on Martin Fowler (_Refactoring_), Kent Beck (_Tidy First?_) and Michael Feathers (_Working
Effectively with Legacy Code_).

## Principles

- A refactoring changes structure, never behaviour. The tests are green before and after, and no
  assertion changes (a move may rewrite import and `vi.mock` paths in tests, nothing else).
- Keep structural and behavioural changes in separate commits. A structural change of more than
  about 200 changed lines, or any move across contexts, gets its own PR, because features are
  squash-merged.
- "Make the change easy, then make the easy change." When a behaviour change is hard, tidy first,
  in a separate commit.
- Take small steps. After each one the code compiles and the focused tests pass.
- Common tidyings: guard clause, extract function, explaining variable or constant, rename,
  reorder for reading, delete dead code, delete a redundant comment.

## Before splitting a large file

Pin its current behaviour with characterization or approval tests at its public surface: store
actions, rendered output, route responses. Their names start with `characterization:`. The first
candidates are the deck store, the builder page, `DeckWizard`, the playtest engine and the AI
routes (`docs/adr/0005-testing-strategy-fakes-contracts-and-characterization.md`). A
characterization test that pins a known bug says so in its name.

## Moves

A move PR changes no logic. It rewrites every import and `vi.mock` path in the same commit. Once
the move codemod exists it makes the moves; re-export shims live only within one series of PRs.

## Never

- Change behaviour "while you are there". A behaviour change found during a refactor gets its own
  `fix/` PR with a Red test.
- Edit a file that an open PR or a pending branch changes, unless the task says so; list the
  overlap instead.
- Delete code that has product intent. Delete only code with no importer and no product intent;
  list orphaned features for the owner.
